{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n\nRule\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n\nKey insight\n- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1478ab18\" lines=\"35-43\">\n<description>\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\nRule\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e2bad24\" lines=\"91-106\">\n<description>\nExtend the diagonal blue ray through the grid, reflecting it off red line-segment mirrors.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one diagonal 8-connected blue ray seed.\n- Red foreground objects, when present, are straight horizontal or vertical mirror segments.\n- Other foreground bars are transparent to the ray and may be overwritten where the ray crosses them.\n\nRule\n- Start from the ray seed endpoint whose outward diagonal extension remains inside the grid.\n- Paint the ray continuously in that diagonal direction until it leaves the grid.\n- If the next cell is a red mirror, do not paint it; reflect by flipping the row direction on a horizontal mirror or the column direction on a vertical mirror.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-red bars are decoys: only red bars reflect the diagonal path, while all other crossed cells become blue.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9968a131\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nShift alternating two-color row bars so each row aligns to the topmost bar's color columns.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background surrounds horizontal two-pixel bars.\n- Every foreground row has exactly one adjacent bar using the same two colors.\n- The topmost bar gives the reference left/right column for each color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep bars whose left color is already in its reference column.\n- For a reversed bar, shift the whole two-pixel bar horizontally until its\n  left color lands in that color's reference column.\n- Background cells vacated by shifted bars become background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The rows are not recolored; each two-color bar is translated so equal\n  colors line up with the topmost reference bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6e50e54\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nMove each maroon marker toward the blue rectangle and recolor the rectangle red.\n\nInput structure\nA background canvas contains one solid blue rectangular object and several isolated\nmaroon marker pixels. Each marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the\nrectangle and lies outside it.\n\nRule\nStart with a background canvas, repaint the blue rectangle red, and move each\nmaroon marker straight toward the rectangle along its row or column. Exactly one\nmarker is strictly closer to the rectangle than all others and enters the\nrectangle boundary; every other marker lands in the adjacent exterior cell just\noutside its aligned side.\n\nKey insight\nThe single entering marker is the unique global nearest marker to the rectangle,\nnot the nearest marker on each of the four sides.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9385bd28\" lines=\"71-87\">\n<description>\nFill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.\n- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.\n- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.\n\nRule:\n- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.\n- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.\n- Fill the diamond (all shortest-path cells between those two corners) with the legend's fill color.\n- Solid 2×2 marker blocks are simply recolored to the fill color.\n- Later legend entries are protected from earlier fills.\n\nKey insight: The L-shaped anchors sit on the marker diagonal; the fill goes on the opposite diagonal of the bounding box.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f8de559\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nErase the first border pixel hit by extending the red marker away from its pink trail.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds a unique red marker and a four-pixel\n  pink trail.\n- The pink pixels form one straight 8-connected ray starting next to the\n  red marker.\n- In the opposite direction from that trail, the red marker faces a\n  non-background border or obstacle pixel beyond background cells.\n\nRule\n- Keep the whole grid fixed except for the first non-background pixel on\n  the ray from the red marker away from the pink trail.\n- Recolor that one hit pixel to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The pink trail marks where the red marker came from, so the edit is found\n  by ray-casting from the red marker in the opposite direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8fff9e47\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nTurn a 4-cell-wide strip of 2x2 blocks into four nested corner-fill patterns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an even-sized rectangle whose shorter dimension is four pixels.\nIt can be partitioned exactly into 2x2 blocks, giving one block for each layer\nof the output quadrants.\n\nRule\nRead the 2x2 blocks in reverse row-major order: bottom-right block first,\nmoving left, then moving upward. For each block, send its top-left,\ntop-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right pixels to the layer sequences of the\nupper-left, upper-right, lower-left, and lower-right output quadrants.\n\nKey insight\nEach input 2x2 block supplies the four colors for one concentric layer, and\nthe layer order is the block order from bottom-right back to top-left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"800d221b\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor a path-network by adjacent seed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Path color P forms a thin orthogonal network with a solid square hub (NxN).\n- Seed colors appear in colored regions (non-bg, non-P) adjacent to path branches.\n\nRule:\n- Infer P as the non-bg color whose largest component is most network-like.\n- Find the largest solid k×k all-P square (the hub); exclude it from recoloring.\n- Recolor each path branch to the seed-region color it touches most.\n- Recolor the hub center pixel to the seed whose branches touch the hub most.\n\nKey insight: The hub stays uncolored; branches inherit color from adjacent seed regions by adjacency count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fbf15a0b\" lines=\"49-69\">\n<description>\nCrop out the grid half containing the two aligned marker pixels, then erase the markers to background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one non-background marker color\n  that appears as exactly two cells.\n- The marker cells are aligned either in one row or one column and both lie\n  in the same half along that direction.\n- Other non-background pixels are content inside one or both halves.\n\nRule\n- If the markers share a row, keep the left or right half whose columns\n  contain them.\n- If the markers share a column, keep the top or bottom half whose rows\n  contain them.\n- In the kept half, recolor the marker pixels to the background color.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
